      <!-- IMU NOISE IS MEASURED, NOT INHERITED.
           Every number in the noise blocks below was derived from a real
           Matrice 4E: rosbag bag_000026, 184 s, from the 6.3 s the aircraft
           sat on the ground before takeoff. tools/imu_from_bag.py does the
           measuring and rewrites this block; do not hand-edit it, re-run the
           tool. The full derivation is in reports/IMU_CALIBRATION.md.

           Three things about these values are not obvious.

           stddev is quoted AT 250 Hz, this sensor's update_rate, but the
           aircraft publishes at 50 Hz. A standard deviation means nothing
           without the rate it was sampled at, so the measurement was turned
           into a noise density and back up: stddev = density * sqrt(250).
           Change update_rate and these become wrong; re-run the tool.

           The density came from the flat top of the spectrum, not from the
           variance of the stationary segment. A parked aircraft sways on its
           legs, and on the x accelerometer that sway is 2.4x the white noise.
           Counting it as sensor noise would have made this model noisier than
           the part it represents.

           The accelerometer means are zero on purpose. DJI subtracts gravity
           from acceleration_body_raw before publishing it, so its mean mixes
           sensor bias with gravity removal error and is not a bias we can
           copy. The gyro means ARE real measured biases.

           dynamic_bias_stddev and dynamic_bias_correlation_time are still the
           inherited x500 figures: 6.3 s of stillness cannot support a 300 s
           correlation time, and a fitted number there would be invention. -->
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>-0.00056509389</mean>
                <stddev>0.00066325603</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>3.22e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.00088365378</mean>
                <stddev>0.00060542074</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>7.65e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>-0.00042490432</mean>
                <stddev>0.00060836685</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>5.37e-05</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>300</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0022675357</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>0.00147</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>100</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0025002416</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>0.000975</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>100</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0</mean>
                <stddev>0.011341797</stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_stddev>0.00107</dynamic_bias_stddev>
                <dynamic_bias_correlation_time>100</dynamic_bias_correlation_time>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </linear_acceleration>


        </imu>
      </sensor>

      <!-- THE HORIZONTAL STDDEV BELOW IS IN DEGREES, NOT METRES.
           SDFormat documents position_sensing/horizontal/noise/stddev as
           metres, but gz-sensors applies it to latitude and longitude
           directly, i.e. in degrees (gazebosim/gz-sensors issue #325). The
           4.5e-6 here is chosen against the implementation, not the spec:
           4.5e-6 deg x 111320 m/deg ~= 0.50 m, which is the sigma_p = 0.5 m
           that Cremona et al. (arXiv:2307.12836) use to model a conventional
           GNSS receiver, and what demo_gnss_stereo_inertial's GNSS factor is
           tuned against.

           IF GAZEBO EVER FIXES #325 THIS BECOMES 4.5 MICROMETRES and the
           simulated GNSS turns into a perfect position source overnight,
           silently. The symptom would be localization error collapsing to
           near zero and the GNSS residual going flat. Check this first.

           Vertical stddev really is metres: 1.0 m, deliberately worse than
           horizontal, as real GNSS altitude is. The paper notes the same
           asymmetry and an altitude bias it leaves to future work. -->
      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
        <navsat>
          <position_sensing>
            <horizontal>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>4.5e-6</stddev>
              </noise>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>1.0</stddev>
              </noise>
            </vertical>
          </position_sensing>
          <velocity_sensing>
            <horizontal>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.05</stddev>
              </noise>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.1</stddev>
              </noise>
            </vertical>
          </velocity_sensing>
        </navsat>
      </sensor>

            <!-- ===================================================================
           THE PERCEPTION LENSES: SIX, SHARED BETWEEN FIVE DIRECTIONS
           ===================================================================
           DJI publishes "six HD low-light fisheye vision sensors" and an
           omnidirectional BINOCULAR system covering five directions. Six
           lenses cannot give five directions two lenses each, so the lenses
           are shared, and the published ranges say how:

             forward / backward   0.4 to 22.5 m     shorter baseline
             lateral              0.5 to 32 m       LONGER baseline
             downward             0.3 to 18.8 m

           A longer range needs a longer baseline. The lateral pair is
           therefore a FRONT lens paired with a REAR lens, whose separation
           along the airframe exceeds the side to side separation of the two
           front lenses. Four lenses at the corners, two underneath:

             FRONT = fl + fr      baseline 0.26 m   (side to side)
             REAR  = rl + rr      baseline 0.26 m
             LEFT  = fl + rl      baseline 0.37 m   (nose to tail)
             RIGHT = fr + rr      baseline 0.37 m
             DOWN  = dl + dr      baseline 0.10 m

           0.37 / 0.26 = 1.42, and 32 / 22.5 = 1.42. The ORDERING is derived;
           the absolute values are chosen to fit the airframe and are not from
           DJI, who do not publish them. Stereo depth scales with baseline, so
           a node calibrated here must be recalibrated against the aircraft.

           WHAT THE PREVIOUS MODEL GOT WRONG. It had seven sensors: a forward
           pair, a rear pair, and a SINGLE camera each for left, right and
           down. A single camera cannot serve start_perception, which must
           publish a left and a right stream for whichever direction is
           chosen. Those three directions could never have worked.

           WHY THE CORNER LENSES ARE YAWED 45 DEGREES OUTWARD. Each one has to
           serve two directions at once: fl is the left eye of FRONT and also
           an eye of LEFT. Pointing it diagonally, with a fisheye wide enough
           to cover from the nose round to the beam, is the only arrangement
           that does both. This is why they must be fisheyes and not pinholes:
           at 190 degrees a rectilinear projection does not exist.

           PROJECTION: equidistant, r = f * theta, the standard fisheye model
           and the one Kalibr calls "equidistant". The previous model used
           Gazebo's default rectilinear projection even for the 160 degree
           downward lens, where it is not merely inaccurate but undefined.

           Equidistant focal length is f = w / hfov:
             corners   704 / 3.3161 = 212.3 px   (190 deg)
             downward  704 / 2.7925 = 252.1 px   (160 deg)
           These are published in config/perception_calib.yaml rather than in
           an SDF intrinsics block, which describes a pinhole and would be
           read as one. The aircraft never populates
           perception_camera_parameters, so a file is also what hardware uses.

           ON base_link WITH A POSE, NOT ON LINKS OF THEIR OWN. A new link
           needs an inertial block, and mass there changes the vehicle's total
           mass and inertia, both tuned in this file and in the 4900_gz_m4e
           airframe.

           FORMAT: the aircraft publishes mono8 704x704 at 20 Hz
           (psdk-ros2 MANIFOLD3_FOXY_PSDK316_SETUP.md), and the WRAPPER TOPIC
           still does. These sensors render R8G8B8 anyway, because
           gz-sensors' WideAngleCameraSensor supports exactly one pixel format
           (RGB_INT8) and errors out on L8 with "Unsupported pixel format [1]".
           A fisheye projection is worth more than saving the conversion, so
           the bridge converts RGB to mono8 on the way out
           (converters.stereo_mono_image) and a consumer sees what the
           aircraft sends. Rendering cost is counted in pixels and unchanged;
           bus cost inside gz is 3x, which is why these stay lazy.

           ORIGINAL NOTE, still true of the aircraft: mono8 704x704 at 20 Hz
           (psdk-ros2 MANIFOLD3_FOXY_PSDK316_SETUP.md). The previous model used
           640x640 for the downward camera, which had no stated source; all six
           are now the documented size.

           Rendering is demand driven: compose_sim.py emits every camera row lazy,
           so with no subscriber gz renders nothing. (config/bridge.yaml is a
           reference copy; the file ros_gz_bridge reads is generated.)

           Source: DJI Matrice 4 Series specifications, enterprise.dji.com.
           The corner layout is an INFERENCE from those figures; DJI does not
           document the physical placement. See reports/CAMERA_ARCHITECTURE.md
           =================================================================== -->

      <sensor name="perception_fl" type="wideanglecamera">
        <pose> 0.185  0.13 0 0 0 0.7854</pose>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>20</update_rate>
        <topic>/drone/perception/fl/image_raw</topic>
        <gz_frame_id>perception_fl</gz_frame_id>
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>3.3161</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>704</width>
            <height>704</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>200.0</far>
          </clip>
          <lens>
            <type>equidistant</type>
            <scale_to_hfov>true</scale_to_hfov>
            <cutoff_angle>1.6581</cutoff_angle>
            <env_texture_size>512</env_texture_size>
          </lens>
        </camera>
      </sensor>

      <sensor name="perception_fr" type="wideanglecamera">
        <pose> 0.185 -0.13 0 0 0 -0.7854</pose>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>20</update_rate>
        <topic>/drone/perception/fr/image_raw</topic>
